leap71/PicoGKRuntime @ 0f26321c (26.2): bulk mesh transfer in the C ABI.

Proposal only. picovoxel, an unofficial community TypeScript/WebAssembly binding
of PicoGK (not affiliated with or endorsed by LEAP 71), ships the same code as a
separate translation unit (src/pico-bulk.cpp) so its vendored runtime stays
unmodified. This file is that code shaped as a diff against upstream.

This is the C ABI half only. The C# half belongs to leap71/PicoGK, a different
repository: Mesh.vVertices()/vTriangles() and Mesh.AddVertices would read and
write through the four new exports instead of looping over Mesh_GetVertex,
Mesh_GetTriangle and Mesh_nAddVertex. It is described here rather than diffed,
for the maintainers to take or adapt.

Why: the ABI moves mesh data one element per call. Reading a 174k-vertex mesh
costs about 522k crossings, and on managed and wasm hosts the crossing
dominates. In picovoxel's WebAssembly build the same read takes 2 crossings and
runs about 150x faster (Apple M2 Pro). The same exports serve C#, Python and any
other binding.

Signatures follow the existing caller-supplied-buffer precedent
(Voxels_GetZSlice, PicoGK.h:341). Reads clamp to the caller's buffer and return
the count written; writes return the index of the first appended element, or -1
on bad input, and go through Mesh::nAddVertex/nAddTriangle so the bounding box
and nMemUsage bookkeeping stay correct.

Layout note: Vector3 and Triangle are 12-byte #pragma pack(1) types identical
in layout to PKVector3/PKTriangle. Vector3 has a user-provided copy constructor
(PicoGKTypes.h:146), so std::copy and not memcpy; clang lowers it to the same
vectorised move at -O3.

Output values: unchanged. picovoxel's suite checks bulk against per-element
transfer byte for byte, including a 100k-vertex FNV-1a comparison
(test/bulk-mesh.test.mjs).

Applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` to a pristine PicoGKRuntime 0f26321c tree.
Not applied by picovoxel's build; see upstream/README.md.

